    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Seam Rip should exile Stormchaser's Talent (Level 2 Class enchantment) rather than a 1/1 Otter Token. Stormchaser's Talent is the engine generating value — it already retrieved a spell from the graveyard at level 2, and if it reaches level 3, it will create an Otter token every time an instant or sorcery is cast. The Otter Token is just a 1/1 prowess creature that can be easily dealt with. Exiling the Talent removes the recurring threat and denies the opponent their primary card advantage/token generation engine.",
      "actionTaken": "Exiled Otter Token with Seam Rip's enter-the-battlefield trigger.",
      "betterLine": "Exile Stormchaser's Talent with Seam Rip to remove the opponent's key enchantment engine before it can reach level 3 and generate overwhelming value.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 116,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 73
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Jeskai Revelation before playing Mistrise Village. Since Steam Vents is an Island Mountain, Mistrise Village would enter untapped, giving 8 mana total. Playing the land first leaves 1 extra mana available after casting Jeskai Revelation, which could be relevant for casting a 1-mana spell drawn off the Revelation (like Spell Snare or similar).",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Jeskai Revelation with 7 lands, leaving Mistrise Village unplayed",
      "betterLine": "Play Mistrise Village first (enters untapped due to Steam Vents being a Mountain), then cast Jeskai Revelation with 8 mana available, leaving 1 mana open after resolution to potentially use on drawn cards",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 128,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 79
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Activated Mistrise Village's 'can't be countered' ability while the opponent had all lands tapped, meaning there was zero chance of the opponent countering anything. This wasted 1 mana ({U} activation cost) for no benefit.",
      "actionTaken": "Paid {U} and tapped Mistrise Village to make the next spell uncounterable, despite the opponent being completely tapped out.",
      "betterLine": "Simply tap Mistrise Village for {U} mana normally, preserving the extra {U} that was spent on the activation cost for other uses or to hold up more interaction.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 143,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 91
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Consult the Star Charts before playing Restless Anchorage means looking at 7 cards instead of 8. Since Consult looks at X cards where X is the number of lands you control, playing the land first (even though it enters tapped) increases the selection pool by 1 card, giving a better chance of finding the best card.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Consult the Star Charts with only 7 lands in play",
      "betterLine": "Play Restless Anchorage first (enters tapped but still counts as a land), then cast Consult the Star Charts to look at 8 cards instead of 7",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 143,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 91
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Passed the turn without playing Riverpyre Verge (missing a land drop) and without casting any spells despite having 3 untapped mana and playable cards like Duelist of the Mind (1U). The reasoning about 'three Quantum Riddlers presenting lethal' appears completely disconnected from the actual board state. Missing the land drop is especially costly when holding two 5-mana Quantum Riddlers that need mana development.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed priority without playing any cards or lands in postcombat main phase.",
      "betterLine": "Play Riverpyre Verge (free land drop), then cast Duelist of the Mind for 1U to develop the board. This advances toward casting Quantum Riddler on future turns while adding a flying vigilance threat.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 157,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 95
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "With 8 lands already on the battlefield, the 9th land (Riverpyre Verge) provides diminishing returns. A second Duelist of the Mind provides redundancy against removal and is a 2-mana flying threat that pairs well with the card-draw heavy gameplan. The opponent has Seam Rip available and Get Lost in graveyard, so having a backup threat matters.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Duelist of the Mind, keeping Riverpyre Verge (a 9th land)",
      "betterLine": "Discard Riverpyre Verge instead - at 8 lands, an extra land is near-worthless, while a second Duelist provides threat redundancy and is efficiently costed at 2 mana as a flying clock",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 160,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 96
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast kicked Consult the Star Charts before playing a land drop. With 7 lands, they looked at 7 cards. Playing a land first would have given 8 lands, looking at 8 cards for better selection quality - strictly better with no downside since they had a land to play.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast kicked Consult the Star Charts with 7 lands on battlefield, looking at only 7 cards",
      "betterLine": "Play one of the available lands first (Cori Mountain Monastery or Mistrise Village enter untapped), then cast kicked Consult the Star Charts looking at 8 cards for improved selection",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 161,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 97
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "MiMo Hothead declined to play Restless Anchorage as their land for the turn. Since it enters tapped, there's no advantage to waiting - playing it immediately gives access to an extra mana source next turn and a creature-land activation. Additionally, Consult the Star Charts is an instant that should be saved for the opponent's end step to keep No More Lies mana open, so that part of the pass is fine.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed priority without playing Restless Anchorage or casting any spells.",
      "betterLine": "Play Restless Anchorage (enters tapped, pure upside) and hold Consult the Star Charts and No More Lies for the opponent's turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 169,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 99
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Choosing Temur for Frostcliff Siege gives creatures +1/+0, trample, and haste, but KimiK2 Spike currently has no creatures on the battlefield (only lands, Stormchaser's Talent enchantment, and a Map Token). With Jeskai, whenever creatures deal combat damage to a player, you draw a card - this synergizes perfectly with the Stormchaser's Talent (which at level 3 creates prowess Otter tokens when casting instants/sorceries) and the Duelist of the Mind in hand (whose power equals cards drawn this turn). Jeskai would provide sustained card advantage that compounds with the deck's draw-heavy strategy, while Temur provides a minor stat buff to creatures that don't yet exist. The +1/+0 and haste are marginal when future creatures (Otters, Duelist) are small, while drawing extra cards fuels Duelist's power and finds more spells for Otter generation.",
      "actionTaken": "Chose Temur mode for Frostcliff Siege, giving creatures +1/+0, trample, and haste.",
      "betterLine": "Choose Jeskai mode for Frostcliff Siege to draw cards on combat damage, synergizing with Duelist of the Mind (power = cards drawn) and Stormchaser's Talent Otter token generation for a much stronger long-term game plan.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 171,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 100
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Consult the Star Charts has mana value 2. KimiK2 Spike has two Spell Snares in hand, which counter spells with mana value 2. The opponent is resolving Consult the Star Charts, which would let them dig through their library for key cards. Countering it with Spell Snare costs only 1 mana and would deny the opponent card selection/advantage. KimiK2 Spike has two copies so using one still leaves a backup. Letting the opponent resolve a card selection spell when you have a free counter for it is a meaningful mistake.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to cast Spell Snare on Consult the Star Charts, letting it resolve.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Spell Snare targeting Consult the Star Charts to counter it and deny the opponent card advantage/selection.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 176,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 106
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Declined to counter Consult the Star Charts (mana value 2) with Spell Snare, allowing the opponent to dig through 7-8 cards and find a key card. KimiK2 Spike had TWO copies of Spell Snare in hand, so using one here would still leave a backup for later threats. The reasoning about 'THREE Quantum Riddlers for 12 damage' appears completely disconnected from the actual board state (only one Quantum Riddler in hand, no creatures on board). The opponent subsequently found useful cards and attacked with Monk Tokens.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to cast Spell Snare on Consult the Star Charts, letting the opponent resolve a powerful card selection spell for free.",
      "betterLine": "Counter Consult the Star Charts with one Spell Snare, denying the opponent significant card selection while retaining the second Spell Snare for future 2-mana threats.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 177,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 109
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "At 4 life with the opponent at 12 life, casting Quantum Riddler with Warp for only {1}{U} instead of its full cost {3}{U}{U} means it will be exiled at the next end step. This wastes a 4/6 flying body that could serve as a crucial blocker when at critically low life. The player's reasoning about 'riot haste' and 'Stormchaser's Talent for haste' is confused - neither of those things apply here. Quantum Riddler has no haste, so it can't attack this turn anyway when cast with Warp, and it will be exiled at end of turn. The player still draws a card from the ETB trigger (plus an extra card due to the one-or-fewer cards in hand ability not applying since they have 6 cards), but they lose a permanent 4/6 flying blocker that could protect their 4 life total. With 6 lands available, they could cast it for full cost and keep it permanently.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Quantum Riddler with Warp for {1}{U}, meaning it will be exiled at end of turn.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Quantum Riddler for its full cost of {3}{U}{U} using 5 of their 6 available lands, keeping the 4/6 flyer permanently as both a threat and a critical blocker at 4 life. This leaves 1 mana open, and they still have Spell Snare in hand for protection.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 183,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 115
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "At 2 life, KimiK2 Spike declined to block a 1/1 Monk Token with their Otter Token, dropping to 1 life. At such a critically low life total, every point matters - going to 1 life means any burn spell, any unblocked creature, or any incidental damage source kills them. The Otter could have blocked and at minimum traded with the Monk (both 1/1), or if Frostcliff Siege was on Temur mode, the 2/1 Otter would kill the Monk and survive.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to block the Monk Token, taking 1 damage and dropping from 2 life to 1 life.",
      "betterLine": "Block the Monk Token with the Otter Token to preserve the critical life point at 2 life. Even trading the Otter for the Monk is worthwhile when at 2 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 217,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 133
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "KimiK2 Spike cast Fear of Missing Out before playing their land drop (Riverpyre Verge in hand) and before combat. Playing the land first would leave 5 mana open after casting FoMO instead of 4, which matters when holding two Spell Snares at 1 life. Additionally, casting a creature pre-combat when they could attack first with existing creatures and potentially draw off Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai) is suboptimal sequencing.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Fear of Missing Out in precombat main phase without playing Riverpyre Verge first",
      "betterLine": "Play Riverpyre Verge first, then attack with any existing creatures to potentially trigger Frostcliff Siege's Jeskai draw, then cast Fear of Missing Out in second main phase with maximum information and mana available",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 225,
      "player": "KimiK2 Spike",
      "decisionIndex": 144
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "MiMo used Get Lost on an Otter Token when they have Day of Judgment in hand to handle creatures. The enchantments Frostcliff Siege (giving +1/+0, trample, haste to all creatures) and Stormchaser's Talent (the token-generating engine) are much higher-value targets that can't be answered by Day of Judgment. Additionally, destroying a token gives the opponent two Map tokens for minimal board impact.",
      "actionTaken": "Targeted an Otter Token with Get Lost, giving the opponent two Map tokens while having Day of Judgment available for creatures.",
      "betterLine": "Target Frostcliff Siege (removes the power buff, trample, and haste from all creatures, neutering the opponent's board) or Stormchaser's Talent (stops future token generation). Then use Day of Judgment to clean up the remaining creature threats.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 229,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 149
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 1 life and MiMo has no creatures or burn spells. Restless Anchorage is the only visible way to deal the final point of damage by animating it into a 2/3 flyer. Choosing Mistrise Village instead means MiMo has no win condition on board.",
      "actionTaken": "Chose Mistrise Village as land drop",
      "betterLine": "Play Restless Anchorage to have a win condition. Next turn, animate it for {1}{W}{U} and attack for lethal against the opponent at 1 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 230,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 150
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "MiMo passed their precombat main phase with the opponent at 1 life, missing a land drop (had Cori Mountain Monastery and Mistrise Village in choices) and failing to cast Stock Up to dig for any damage source to close out the game. The opponent at 1 life means any creature or damage source wins immediately.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed the precombat main phase without playing a land or casting any spells",
      "betterLine": "Play Mistrise Village (or Cori Mountain Monastery for its card exile ability), then cast Stock Up to dig for a threat like Restless Anchorage or any creature that can deal the final 1 damage to close the game",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 237,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 156
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Opponent is at 1 life and cast Three Steps Ahead (draw mode) to dig for answers/threats. MiMo had No More Lies available and the opponent only had 2 untapped mana (couldn't pay the {3} tax), making it a hard counter. Letting a desperate opponent at 1 life draw extra cards gives them more chances to find a path to victory.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to counter Three Steps Ahead, allowing opponent at 1 life to draw 2 cards and discard 1.",
      "betterLine": "Cast No More Lies to counter Three Steps Ahead, denying the opponent at 1 life crucial card selection. MiMo still had a second No More Lies for future threats.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 241,
      "player": "MiMo Hothead",
      "decisionIndex": 159
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Chose to bounce 'Otter Token' which doesn't appear to exist on the opponent's battlefield. While the bounce target is relatively unimportant since Jeskai Revelation's 4 damage should be lethal against the opponent at 1 life, the player demonstrated confusion about the board state. The optimal bounce target would have been Frostcliff Siege (opponent's most valuable permanent) as insurance.",
      "actionTaken": "Targeted a non-existent Otter Token for the bounce effect of Jeskai Revelation",
      "betterLine": "Bounce Frostcliff Siege (the opponent's most valuable permanent) to maximize value in case something goes wrong with the lethal damage",
